→ Rendering:

    • Efeitos:

        ◦ Múltiplas imagens do disco —  raios que orbitam 1.5 voltas antes de escapar formam uma segunda imagem do disco, mais fina, 
                                        acima e abaixo da primeira. Está implícito na física mas MAX_STEPS atual pode estar cortando esses raios.

        ◦ Bloom/glow —  pós-processamento no host: pixels muito brilhantes do disco sangram luz para os vizinhos. Feito com convolução gaussiana no 
                        buffer de pixels antes de salvar o PNG.
        
        ◦ exploração real das bordas da sombra e dos polos do disco
    

    • Real Time:

        ◦ Reprojection / Frame Extrapolation
            →   When the camera moves slightly, instead of re-rendering from scratch, reproject the previous frame's 
                pixels to the new camera position. Only re-trace pixels that don't reproject cleanly (the photon ring edge, disk boundary). 
                This is exactly what DLSS Frame Generation and AMD FSR3 do. For small mouse movements you'd get the new frame almost for free.

        ◦ TAA (Temporal Anti-Aliasing)
            →   Each frame, jitter the ray by a sub-pixel Halton sequence offset. Blend with previous frame at ~0.1 weight. 
                Over 8-10 frames the image converges to clean supersampled quality. Your will_rerender flag already gives you the reset signal — 
                when camera moves, clear the accumulation buffer. Free SSAA over time with zero per-frame cost.

        ◦ Checkerboard Rendering
            →   Render only odd pixels on odd frames, even pixels on even frames. Reconstruct missing pixels from neighbors. Halves your pixel 
                count per frame. PS4 Pro used this extensively for "4K" output. Easy to implement — just skip pixels where (x + y + frame_count) % 2 != 0 
                and fill from previous frame.

        ◦ Hierarchical Z / Early Depth Rejection
            →   For your case: divide the screen into tiles (e.g. 32x32). Compute the impact parameter range for the tile's corner rays. 
                If the entire tile's b_min > b_crit * 5, skip the whole tile to starmap without per-pixel integration. Amortizes the b calculation across 
                many pixels.

        ◦ Persistent Threads / Work Queue
            →   Instead of one thread per pixel with wildly variable work (some pixels do 50 steps, photon ring pixels do 10000), use a work queue where 
                threads grab the next unfinished pixel. Eliminates warp divergence where fast threads sit idle waiting for the slow photon ring threads in 
                the same warp. This is called "persistent kernels" in CUDA.

        ◦ LOD (Level of Detail) for the Disk
            →   Far from the disk boundary, use fewer integration steps and a simpler emissivity model. Only near the photon ring and disk edge do you need 
                full precision. Games do this with mip maps and LOD bias — your equivalent is a coarser step for rays with r > disk_r2 * 2.

        ◦ DLSS / FSR (Upscaling)
            →   Render at half resolution, upscale with a spatial filter. FSR1 is just a spatial sharpening filter — trivial to implement as a 
                GLSL post-process pass. Render at 400x300, display at 800x600. Combined with your Etapa 2 adaptive resolution this gives you interactive 
                framerates at acceptable quality.
        
        ◦ The highest impact ones for your specific bottleneck, in order: 
            
            persistent threads (fixes warp divergence from photon ring rays), 
                TAA (free quality), 
                    checkerboard (half the work), 
                        tile early exit (extends your impact parameter optimization to whole tiles).


